NNAAMMEE
     llaamm — laminate files

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llaamm [--FF|ff _m_i_n_._m_a_x] [--PP|pp _m_i_n_._m_a_x] [--SS|ss _s_e_p_s_t_r_i_n_g] [--TT|tt _c] _f_i_l_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     llaamm copies the named files side by side onto the standard output.  The
     _n-th input lines from the input _f_i_l_e_s are considered fragments of the
     single long _n-th output line into which they are assembled.  The name “-”
     means the standard input, and may be repeated.

     The options are as follows:

     --FF|ff _m_i_n_._m_a_x  Print line fragments according to the format string
                   _m_i_n_._m_a_x, where _m_i_n is the minimum field width and _m_a_x the
                   maximum field width.  If _m_i_n begins with a zero, zeros will
                   be prepended to make up the field width instead of blanks,
                   and if it begins with a ‘-’, the fragment will be left-ad‐
                   justed within the field.

                   If --ff is used, it affects only the file after it; if --FF is
                   used, it affects all subsequent files until it appears
                   again uncapitalized.

     --PP|pp _m_i_n_._m_a_x  Like --ff, but pad this file's field when end-of-file is
                   reached and other files are still active.

                   If --pp is used, it affects only the file after it; if --PP is
                   used, it affects all subsequent files until it appears
                   again uncapitalized.

     --SS|ss _s_e_p_s_t_r_i_n_g
                   Print _s_e_p_s_t_r_i_n_g before printing line fragments from the
                   next file.  This option may appear after the last file.

                   If --ss is used, it affects only the file after it; if --SS is
                   used, it affects all subsequent files until it appears
                   again uncapitalized.

     --TT|tt _c        The input line terminator is _c instead of a newline.  The
                   newline normally appended to each output line is omitted.

                   If --tt is used, it affects only the file after it; if --TT is
                   used, it affects all subsequent files until it appears
                   again uncapitalized.

     To print files simultaneously for easy viewing use pr(1).

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     LC_CTYPE  The character encoding locale(1).  It determines the display
               widths of characters used by the --ff and --pp options.  If unset
               or set to "C", "POSIX", or an unsupported value, each byte is
               regarded as a character of display width 1.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Join four files together along each line:

           $ lam file1 file2 file3 file4

     Merge the lines from four different files:

           $ lam file1 -S "\
           " file2 file3 file4

     Join every two lines of a file:

           $ lam - - < file

     A form letter with substitutions keyed by ‘@’ can be done with:

           $ lam -t @ letter changes

